Relevant columns and their potential usage for the task  
• "date": filter records from 2022‑01‑01 forward, truncate to months, count months.  
• "category_name": group by liquor category, identify qualifying categories, label results.  
• "volume_sold_liters": measure sales volume, sum per month/category, compute % share of monthly totals.  
• (Optional) "category": alternate code for the same concept – can help if duplicates appear.  
Conditions that are likely to be used  
• WHERE "date" >= '2022‑01‑01' — keep only the required time window.  
• HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT month) >= 24 — keep categories with data in at least 24 months.  
• HAVING AVG("pct_of_month") >= 0.01 — keep categories whose average monthly share ≥ 1 %.  

Ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (every query ends with LIMIT 20).

```sql
-- 1. Description: List 20 different liquor category names that exist in the sales table.
SELECT DISTINCT
       "category_name"
FROM   "IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."SALES"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Show sample rows (after 2022‑01‑01) to verify the main columns we'll use.
SELECT "date",
       "category",
       "category_name",
       "volume_sold_liters"
FROM   "IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."SALES"
WHERE  "date" >= '2022-01-01'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Total liters sold in January‑2022 per category (top 20 by volume).
SELECT DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', "date")          AS "sales_month",
       "category_name",
       SUM("volume_sold_liters")            AS "liters_sold"
FROM   "IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."SALES"
WHERE  "date" >= '2022-01-01'
GROUP  BY 1, 2
HAVING DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', "date") = '2022-01-01'
ORDER  BY "liters_sold" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Compute each month’s grand total liters so we can turn category volumes into percentages.
SELECT DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', "date") AS "sales_month",
       SUM("volume_sold_liters")   AS "total_liters_month"
FROM   "IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."SALES"
WHERE  "date" >= '2022-01-01'
GROUP  BY 1
ORDER  BY 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Monthly percentage share of a single example category (‘VODKA’) to validate math.
WITH month_tot AS (
    SELECT DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', "date") AS "sales_month",
           SUM("volume_sold_liters")   AS "total_liters_month"
    FROM   "IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."SALES"
    WHERE  "date" >= '2022-01-01'
    GROUP  BY 1
)
SELECT m."sales_month",
       s."category_name",
       SUM(s."volume_sold_liters") / m."total_liters_month" AS "pct_of_month"
FROM   "IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."SALES"  s
JOIN   month_tot                                          m
  ON   DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', s."date") = m."sales_month"
WHERE  s."date" >= '2022-01-01'
  AND  s."category_name" ILIKE '%VODKA%'
GROUP  BY 1, 2, m."total_liters_month"
ORDER  BY 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Identify categories that (i) appear in ≥ 24 months and (ii) average ≥ 1 % of monthly liters.
WITH month_tot AS (
    SELECT DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', "date") AS "sales_month",
           SUM("volume_sold_liters")   AS "total_liters_month"
    FROM   "IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."SALES"
    WHERE  "date" >= '2022-01-01'
    GROUP  BY 1
),
cat_month AS (
    SELECT DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', s."date")                            AS "sales_month",
           s."category_name",
           SUM(s."volume_sold_liters") / m."total_liters_month"     AS "pct_of_month"
    FROM   "IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."SALES" s
    JOIN   month_tot m
      ON   DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', s."date") = m."sales_month"
    WHERE  s."date" >= '2022-01-01'
    GROUP  BY 1, 2, m."total_liters_month"
)
SELECT "category_name",
       COUNT(*)                       AS "months_present",
       AVG("pct_of_month")            AS "avg_pct_of_month"
FROM   cat_month
GROUP  BY 1
HAVING COUNT(*) >= 24
   AND AVG("pct_of_month") >= 0.01
ORDER  BY "avg_pct_of_month" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Produce a narrow (month, category) % table for only the qualifying categories from query 6.
WITH month_tot AS (
    SELECT DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', "date") AS "sales_month",
           SUM("volume_sold_liters")   AS "total_liters_month"
    FROM   "IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."SALES"
    WHERE  "date" >= '2022-01-01'
    GROUP  BY 1
),
qual_cat AS (
    /* Re‑use logic of query 6 to find qualifying categories */
    SELECT "category_name"
    FROM (
        SELECT "category_name",
               COUNT(*)                    AS "months_present",
               AVG("pct_of_month")         AS "avg_pct_of_month"
        FROM (
            SELECT DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', s."date")                       AS "sales_month",
                   s."category_name",
                   SUM(s."volume_sold_liters") /
                   SUM(SUM(s."volume_sold_liters")) OVER (PARTITION BY DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', s."date"))
                   AS "pct_of_month"
            FROM   "IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."SALES" s
            WHERE  s."date" >= '2022-01-01'
            GROUP  BY 1, 2
        ) tmp
        GROUP  BY 1
        HAVING COUNT(*) >= 24
           AND AVG("pct_of_month") >= 0.01
    )
),
cat_month AS (
    SELECT DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', s."date")                        AS "sales_month",
           s."category_name",
           SUM(s."volume_sold_liters") /
           SUM(SUM(s."volume_sold_liters")) OVER (PARTITION BY DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', s."date"))
           AS "pct_of_month"
    FROM   "IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."SALES" s
    JOIN   qual_cat q
      ON   s."category_name" = q."category_name"
    WHERE  s."date" >= '2022-01-01'
    GROUP  BY 1, 2
)
SELECT *
FROM   cat_month
ORDER  BY "sales_month", "category_name"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Compute Pearson correlation for every ordered category‑pair using monthly % shares.
WITH cat_month AS (  /* Same CTE as query 7 but pared‑down for clarity */
    SELECT DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', s."date")                       AS "sales_month",
           s."category_name",
           SUM(s."volume_sold_liters") /
           SUM(SUM(s."volume_sold_liters")) OVER (PARTITION BY DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', s."date"))
           AS "pct_of_month"
    FROM   "IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."SALES" s
    WHERE  s."date" >= '2022-01-01'
    GROUP  BY 1, 2
    QUALIFY COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY "category_name") >= 24
       AND AVG("pct_of_month") OVER (PARTITION BY "category_name") >= 0.01
)
SELECT c1."category_name"           AS "cat_a",
       c2."category_name"           AS "cat_b",
       CORR(c1."pct_of_month", c2."pct_of_month") AS "pearson_corr"
FROM   cat_month c1
JOIN   cat_month c2
  ON   c1."sales_month"  = c2."sales_month"
 AND   c1."category_name" < c2."category_name"   -- remove self and dup pairs
GROUP  BY 1, 2
ORDER  BY "pearson_corr" ASC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Same as query 8 but keep only the single pair with the overall minimum correlation.
WITH cat_month AS (
    SELECT DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', s."date")                       AS "sales_month",
           s."category_name",
           SUM(s."volume_sold_liters") /
           SUM(SUM(s."volume_sold_liters")) OVER (PARTITION BY DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', s."date"))
           AS "pct_of_month"
    FROM   "IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."SALES" s
    WHERE  s."date" >= '2022-01-01'
    GROUP  BY 1, 2
    QUALIFY COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY "category_name") >= 24
       AND AVG("pct_of_month") OVER (PARTITION BY "category_name") >= 0.01
),
pair_corr AS (
    SELECT c1."category_name"                       AS "cat_a",
           c2."category_name"                       AS "cat_b",
           CORR(c1."pct_of_month", c2."pct_of_month") AS "pearson_corr"
    FROM   cat_month c1
    JOIN   cat_month c2
      ON   c1."sales_month"  = c2."sales_month"
     AND   c1."category_name" < c2."category_name"
    GROUP  BY 1, 2
)
SELECT *
FROM   pair_corr
ORDER  BY "pearson_corr" ASC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Fetch final answer – the two category names with the very lowest correlation
-- (still limited to 20 rows in case of ties).  Ready for reporting.
WITH cat_month AS (
    SELECT DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', s."date")                       AS "sales_month",
           s."category_name",
           SUM(s."volume_sold_liters") /
           SUM(SUM(s."volume_sold_liters")) OVER (PARTITION BY DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', s."date"))
           AS "pct_of_month"
    FROM   "IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."IOWA_LIQUOR_SALES"."SALES" s
    WHERE  s."date" >= '2022-01-01'
    GROUP  BY 1, 2
    QUALIFY COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY "category_name") >= 24
       AND AVG("pct_of_month") OVER (PARTITION BY "category_name") >= 0.01
),
pair_corr AS (
    SELECT c1."category_name"                       AS "cat_a",
           c2."category_name"                       AS "cat_b",
           CORR(c1."pct_of_month", c2."pct_of_month") AS "pearson_corr"
    FROM   cat_month c1
    JOIN   cat_month c2
      ON   c1."sales_month"  = c2."sales_month"
     AND   c1."category_name" < c2."category_name"
    GROUP  BY 1, 2
),
lowest AS (
    SELECT *, RANK() OVER (ORDER BY "pearson_corr" ASC) AS rnk
    FROM   pair_corr
)
SELECT "cat_a"  AS "category_name_1",
       "cat_b"  AS "category_name_2",
       "pearson_corr"
FROM   lowest
WHERE  rnk = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

These 10 queries gradually explore the required columns, build monthly metrics, apply the 1 % / 24‑month filters, compute correlations for every qualifying category pair, and finally return the pair with the lowest Pearson correlation of their monthly sales‑volume percentages.
